WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.868 --> 00:00:02.934
[ Up-tempo music plays ]

00:00:02.934 --> 00:00:04.467
-Hey, everybody.

00:00:04.467 --> 00:00:06.834
We referred to
Camilla Parker Bowles

00:00:06.834 --> 00:00:08.868
as the queen.

00:00:08.868 --> 00:00:11.634
She is the queen consort.

00:00:11.634 --> 00:00:14.501
"Consort," of course, is
British slang

00:00:14.501 --> 00:00:16.667
[British accent]
for "home-wrecker"!

00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:20.133
[ Laughter ]

00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:22.267
[ Normal voice ] Buffalo wings
are served with blue cheese,

00:00:22.267 --> 00:00:23.567
not ranch dressing,

00:00:23.567 --> 00:00:26.567
but they are always eaten
with regret.

00:00:26.567 --> 00:00:31.367
[ Laughter ]

00:00:31.367 --> 00:00:34.400
I said, if the word
"comptroller"

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.033
was pronounced "controller,"

00:00:38.033 --> 00:00:42.267
then "computer" would be
pronounced "con-puter."

00:00:42.267 --> 00:00:45.100
I blew my own joke.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:47.734
"Joke."
[ Laughter ]

00:00:47.734 --> 00:00:51.367
A bunch of you said,
"If 'comptrol' is 'control,'

00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:54.067
that means the 'M-P' should
be pronounced as an 'N,'

00:00:54.067 --> 00:00:57.267
which means 'computer'
would be con-uter.'"

00:00:57.267 --> 00:00:58.634
[ Laughter ]

00:00:58.634 --> 00:01:01.167
You guys got it right.

00:01:01.167 --> 00:01:04.234
I got it wrong.

00:01:04.234 --> 00:01:06.133
What can I say?

00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:09.100
I'm an ass-t.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.133
[ Laughter ]

00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:13.801
I have an exciting update

00:01:13.801 --> 00:01:16.701
about my soon-to-be-produced

00:01:16.701 --> 00:01:18.968
Broadway show, "Cicada, Cicada."

00:01:18.968 --> 00:01:20.868
[ Laughter ]

00:01:20.868 --> 00:01:22.367
I don't want to give anything
away,

00:01:22.367 --> 00:01:26.601
but the update regards costumes.

00:01:26.601 --> 00:01:31.334
[ Laughter ]

00:01:31.334 --> 00:01:34.200
You do not use quarters
at Dave &amp; Buster's.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.467
You need a card.
I think I have one here.

00:01:36.467 --> 00:01:39.367
There we go.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:39.367 --> 00:01:41.634
[ Sniffles ]

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:44.300
[ Sighs ]

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:47.634
I pronounced
the toilet paper brand

00:01:47.634 --> 00:01:50.968
Charmin "Charr-min"

00:01:50.968 --> 00:01:52.634
in "You're Burnt,"

00:01:52.634 --> 00:01:54.334
which led some of you
to the conclusion

00:01:54.334 --> 00:01:57.534
that I don't use Charmin.

00:01:57.534 --> 00:02:00.534
I must use
an even-fancier toilet paper.

00:02:00.534 --> 00:02:04.234
How dare you?

00:02:04.234 --> 00:02:05.968
[ Laughter ]

00:02:05.968 --> 00:02:08.033
Toi-- Toilet paper.

00:02:08.033 --> 00:02:12.868
I got a bidet filled
with cham-pagne.

00:02:12.868 --> 00:02:15.901
[ Laughter ]

00:02:15.901 --> 00:02:18.901
[ Blows ]

00:02:18.901 --> 00:02:20.934
[ Blows ]

00:02:20.934 --> 00:02:23.734
[ Sighs ]

00:02:23.734 --> 00:02:28.367
[ Laughter ]

00:02:28.367 --> 00:02:31.200
I was told popes can't have kids
because they're celibate.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.467
I didn't know that.

00:02:33.467 --> 00:02:36.801
Also, do they know that?

00:02:36.801 --> 00:02:38.400
[ Laughter ]

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.133
I'm trying to start a...

00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:45.033
"Oc-TAG-o-nal,"
not "oc-ta-GON-al."

00:02:46.834 --> 00:02:48.667
The guy in the clip
said it wrong,

00:02:48.667 --> 00:02:50.033
and then I said it wrong,
and I'm not going to

00:02:50.033 --> 00:02:52.300
wear that one.
Okay?

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.000
[ Laughter ]

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.501
We said,

00:02:56.501 --> 00:02:59.300
you know, there was
Super Bowl ads for Jesus.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:02.534
And we said Jesus didn't need
a Super Bowl commercial,

00:03:02.534 --> 00:03:05.300
because, you know,
He doesn't need marketing.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:08.467
He did one sermon
2,000 years ago,

00:03:08.467 --> 00:03:10.300
and people are still
talking about Him.

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:12.968
We were referring
to the Sermon on the Mount.

00:03:12.968 --> 00:03:17.534
He also did
a Sermon on the Plain,

00:03:17.534 --> 00:03:20.400
which we hear about way less
because, I guess, it was

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.534
just, like,
a terrible sound system.

00:03:22.534 --> 00:03:24.801
[ Laughter ]

00:03:24.801 --> 00:03:27.267
Like, everyone in the back
was like...

00:03:27.267 --> 00:03:30.033
[ Laughter ]

00:03:30.033 --> 00:03:31.634
They had to do a --
I guess there were, like,

00:03:31.634 --> 00:03:33.601
a ton of refunds.

00:03:33.601 --> 00:03:35.033
Don't get me wrong.

00:03:35.033 --> 00:03:36.434
There are still
people out there --

00:03:36.434 --> 00:03:37.834
you know the kind of fans --
they were like,

00:03:37.834 --> 00:03:39.601
"No, the Sermon on the Plain
is the best one."

00:03:39.601 --> 00:03:42.501
And they're like, "Oh, the --"
You know?

00:03:42.501 --> 00:03:46.167
[ Laughter ]
Bandwagon people.

00:03:46.167 --> 00:03:49.667
"They'll sell you on Mount,
but Plain..."

00:03:49.667 --> 00:03:52.100
It's like people
who think, like, "Nebraska"

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.534
is Springsteen's best album.

00:03:54.534 --> 00:03:56.734
Good album, but...

00:03:56.734 --> 00:04:00.200
"Play 'Mansion on the Hill'!"

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:02.033
You know those people
at concerts?

00:04:02.033 --> 00:04:04.367
Like, "Dude, he's not gonna."

00:04:04.367 --> 00:04:06.834
Uh...

00:04:06.834 --> 00:04:08.567
we said Herschel Walker
was famous

00:04:08.567 --> 00:04:10.200
for thinking vampires were cool.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:12.467
A lot of you said he was famous
for playing football.

00:04:12.467 --> 00:04:15.901
And I was like, "Oh, it was
that Herschel Walker?"

00:04:15.901 --> 00:04:18.601
[ Laughter ]

00:04:18.601 --> 00:04:21.934
Explains a lot.

00:04:21.934 --> 00:04:25.567
Oh!
[ Laughter ]

00:04:25.567 --> 00:04:27.100
Not this week, but last week,

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:30.634
we said,
Mike Lindell is so loud,

00:04:30.634 --> 00:04:36.200
you can hear his voice
inside a seashell.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:38.801
When we took out something,
we called it a seashell.

00:04:38.801 --> 00:04:40.901
It was not -- I was corrected --
it was a conch.

00:04:40.901 --> 00:04:43.434
And, um...
[ Laughter ]

00:04:43.434 --> 00:04:46.868
Hollywood, though,

00:04:46.868 --> 00:04:49.567
knows a hit when they see one.

00:04:49.567 --> 00:04:53.400
And, uh, we got clearance

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.801
from the studio

00:04:55.801 --> 00:04:59.133
to make a big announcement
about a children's film.

00:04:59.133 --> 00:05:03.334
Everybody should
keep their eye out for...

00:05:03.334 --> 00:05:05.734
"Lindell, the Shell
With Boots On."

00:05:05.734 --> 00:05:08.734
[ Laughter ]

00:05:14.334 --> 00:05:16.167
[ Grunts ]

00:05:16.167 --> 00:05:20.000
[ Laughter ]

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.400
I said you should never drink
your own urine in the desert.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:24.634
I should have clarified.

00:05:24.634 --> 00:05:26.634
It's totally fine
to drink someone else's.

00:05:26.634 --> 00:05:28.934
[ Laughter ]

00:05:28.934 --> 00:05:31.734
So never drink your-in,
but you can drink their-in.

00:05:31.734 --> 00:05:33.133
[ Laughter ]

00:05:33.133 --> 00:05:36.467
There's a Joycean amount
of wordplay on this show.

00:05:36.467 --> 00:05:38.701
[ Laughter ]

00:05:38.701 --> 00:05:41.267
Joyce is our receptionist.

00:05:41.267 --> 00:05:43.801
[ Laughter ]

00:05:43.801 --> 00:05:46.367
She's a lot.

00:05:46.367 --> 00:05:49.701
"Hey, Seth, how much does
a pirate pay for corn?"

00:05:49.701 --> 00:05:52.868
"Not now, Joyce. Just not now."

00:05:52.868 --> 00:05:55.200
"A buccaneer."

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:57.100
[ Laughter ]

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:59.200
"Do I have any calls?

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:01.300
Did I have any calls today,
Joyce?"

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:04.167
"Oh, it looks like
I knocked the receiver off."

00:06:04.167 --> 00:06:06.200
[ Laughter ]

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:08.167
"Shucks.

00:06:08.167 --> 00:06:09.567
That's what the pirate said

00:06:09.567 --> 00:06:11.467
when he couldn't afford
the corn."

00:06:11.467 --> 00:06:12.968
[ Laughter ]

00:06:12.968 --> 00:06:18.701
I swear, it's lucky
you're Lorne's mistress, Joyce."

00:06:18.701 --> 00:06:21.000
Joking. I'm joking.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:22.434
I got to say I'm joking,

00:06:22.434 --> 00:06:25.801
'cause these go online
at, like, 1:00 a.m.

00:06:25.801 --> 00:06:27.968
Usually get my first text
from Lorne

00:06:27.968 --> 00:06:29.968
about his favorite parts
at, like, 1:15.

00:06:29.968 --> 00:06:34.634
[ Laughter ]

00:06:34.634 --> 00:06:36.767
[ Chuckles ]

00:06:36.767 --> 00:06:39.634
"I thought Nebraska
should have gotten more."

00:06:39.634 --> 00:06:42.901
[ Laughter ]

00:06:45.167 --> 00:06:47.167
[ Blows ]

00:06:47.167 --> 00:06:49.567
I had a cold last week,
and someone suggested,

00:06:49.567 --> 00:06:52.200
when I have a cold,
Wally should fill in,

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:55.400
adding, "You're
not getting any younger.

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:57.601
It would be good to have
someone else with experience

00:06:57.601 --> 00:06:59.601
should something happen to you."

00:06:59.601 --> 00:07:01.601
Good point.

00:07:01.601 --> 00:07:03.434
If I move on, no one better

00:07:03.434 --> 00:07:06.734
to jump-start the youth movement
than Wally.

00:07:06.734 --> 00:07:09.067
[ Laughter ]

00:07:10.667 --> 00:07:14.667
Wally is very -- very healthy,
it should be noted.

00:07:14.667 --> 00:07:18.334
Probably from, like,
making a fortune

00:07:18.334 --> 00:07:21.534
doing this all day.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:21.534 --> 00:07:24.100
He told me, his last physical,
his doctor said

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:27.167
he had the stamina
of a man half his age

00:07:27.167 --> 00:07:30.167
and the rotator cuff
of a thousand-year-old.

00:07:30.167 --> 00:07:32.834
[ Laughter ]

00:07:32.834 --> 00:07:34.968
Speaking of Wally,

00:07:34.968 --> 00:07:37.200
I did not give credit
where credit was due.

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:39.701
We talked about
this piece of art,

00:07:39.701 --> 00:07:41.968
where the joke was
that Mac Tonight

00:07:41.968 --> 00:07:44.567
had impregnated my wife.
And I didn't notice

00:07:44.567 --> 00:07:46.267
that Wally's in the back
of this photo,

00:07:46.267 --> 00:07:50.434
holding up a cue card
that says, "Mac Roolz."

00:07:50.434 --> 00:07:53.601
-[ Chuckles ]

00:07:53.601 --> 00:07:57.901
-Also, someone wrote
in the comments,

00:07:57.901 --> 00:08:00.701
"Seth's wife is hot."

00:08:00.701 --> 00:08:03.133
Very nice. True.

00:08:03.133 --> 00:08:05.334
Couldn't leave it at that,
though.

00:08:05.334 --> 00:08:08.534
The jackals.

00:08:08.534 --> 00:08:11.567
The joke that completely
ruined the sentiment

00:08:11.567 --> 00:08:13.767
with the next thing they wrote,
and it was only one word.

00:08:13.767 --> 00:08:16.901
They wrote one word
that completely ruined my high

00:08:16.901 --> 00:08:19.701
from "Seth's wife is hot."

00:08:19.701 --> 00:08:22.000
Want to guess
what the one word was?

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:24.701
"Why?"
[ Laughter ]

00:08:28.067 --> 00:08:30.467
[ Blows ]

00:08:30.467 --> 00:08:32.100
[ Laughter ]

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:33.767
We've received

00:08:33.767 --> 00:08:36.434
a lot of really incredible
Mac Tonight fan art,

00:08:36.434 --> 00:08:39.067
to the point that if you were
working on something at home,

00:08:39.067 --> 00:08:43.501
I'm pretty sure you'd want it
to be very good

00:08:43.501 --> 00:08:45.334
before you sent it in.

00:08:45.334 --> 00:08:47.601
We've seen some
real craftsmanship thus far.

00:08:47.601 --> 00:08:50.868
On the other hand,

00:08:50.868 --> 00:08:53.801
maybe like, "I'll mail it."

00:08:53.801 --> 00:08:55.901
[ Laughter ]

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:00.367
Made me so happy.

00:09:00.367 --> 00:09:03.200
I want to thank
whoever sent that in.

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:06.868
Oh, we, um -- "A Closer Look,"

00:09:06.868 --> 00:09:10.167
we had two jokes about Joe Biden
quoting Taylor Swift lyrics,

00:09:10.167 --> 00:09:11.934
and a lot of people were upset

00:09:11.934 --> 00:09:14.067
that we did not follow
the rule of threes,

00:09:14.067 --> 00:09:15.934
the age-old rule in comedy

00:09:15.934 --> 00:09:18.534
that you should
call back something twice

00:09:18.534 --> 00:09:20.133
to make it a total of three.

00:09:20.133 --> 00:09:22.133
And someone said,
"The rule of twos --

00:09:22.133 --> 00:09:25.133
you should try to bring
the rule of twos into fashion."

00:09:25.133 --> 00:09:27.334
And they made me this.

00:09:27.334 --> 00:09:30.400
This is a 2-inch rule.
That's the rule of twos.

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:32.100
[ Laughter ]

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:34.200
I'm going to put that over here.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:38.601
Rule of threes, for example --

00:09:38.601 --> 00:09:41.634
we've done that...

00:09:41.634 --> 00:09:43.834
severed doll-head thing
three times.

00:09:43.834 --> 00:09:46.367
So that's over.

00:09:46.367 --> 00:09:48.534
[ Laughter ]

00:09:51.167 --> 00:09:53.133
[ Sniffles ]

00:09:53.133 --> 00:09:57.267
A gentleman named Mark sent me
a wedding invitation.

00:09:57.267 --> 00:10:00.534
He's marrying, uh,

00:10:00.534 --> 00:10:03.901
who, he made it sound,
a beautiful woman named Jessica,

00:10:03.901 --> 00:10:08.100
who, um, is a real fan
of "Corrections."

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:11.801
He said, "Look, I know you can't
make it to my wedding.

00:10:11.801 --> 00:10:13.334
It would be really nice.
It'd mean a lot to her

00:10:13.334 --> 00:10:15.434
if you would mention us on air."

00:10:15.434 --> 00:10:17.868
And I will say,
a few too many times,

00:10:17.868 --> 00:10:20.868
he said things like,

00:10:20.868 --> 00:10:23.334
"She thinks
you're a comedic genius."

00:10:23.334 --> 00:10:25.167
And then, in parentheses,
he'd write,

00:10:25.167 --> 00:10:27.167
"Her words, not mine."

00:10:27.167 --> 00:10:29.267
[ Laughter ]

00:10:33.634 --> 00:10:37.300
Oh, I -- I, uh, stumbled
on Bill Clinton's name.

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:39.167
A lot of people thought
I said "Bill Kitten."

00:10:39.167 --> 00:10:40.634
[ Laughter ]

00:10:40.634 --> 00:10:42.634
Remember our short-lived
animated show on Hulu?

00:10:42.634 --> 00:10:45.133
"Bill Kitten"?

00:10:45.133 --> 00:10:48.334
It wasn't -- It had a moment.

00:10:48.334 --> 00:10:50.334
[ Laughter ]

00:10:50.334 --> 00:10:53.567
"I did not put my claws
on that ottoman."

00:10:53.567 --> 00:10:55.767
[ Laughter ]

00:10:55.767 --> 00:10:59.701
Pretty good segue
to "Animal Flubs," guys.

00:10:59.701 --> 00:11:01.200
[ Laughter ]

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:02.868
Probably should have cut
"Bill Kitten,"

00:11:02.868 --> 00:11:04.267
but it was the segue.

00:11:04.267 --> 00:11:06.100
[ Sniffles ]

00:11:06.100 --> 00:11:08.601
Can't just do "Animal Flubs"
cold.

00:11:08.601 --> 00:11:10.267
[ Laughter ]

00:11:10.267 --> 00:11:12.767
Before we get to "Animal Flubs,"
this is very embarrassing.

00:11:12.767 --> 00:11:17.434
There was a flub
in the opening song,

00:11:17.434 --> 00:11:21.067
and that's just --
to have a flub in a song

00:11:21.067 --> 00:11:24.234
that's all about
addressing flubs,

00:11:24.234 --> 00:11:26.234
well, you can't stand for that.

00:11:26.234 --> 00:11:30.601
In the -- In the song,
the animals spell out "flubs,"

00:11:30.601 --> 00:11:32.901
and the eagle says
the letter "B."

00:11:32.901 --> 00:11:36.167
And a bunch of you said,
"An eagle doesn't have lips

00:11:36.167 --> 00:11:38.534
and couldn't make a 'B' sound."

00:11:38.534 --> 00:11:41.200
And it was just
so gutting to read,

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:43.968
'cause the rest of it holds up.

00:11:43.968 --> 00:11:47.200
[ Laughter ]

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:50.467
Anyway, we went back
in the studio, we rerecorded it.

00:11:50.467 --> 00:11:52.434
See if you can
tell the difference.

00:11:52.434 --> 00:11:55.200
-♪ Animal Flubs, Animal Flubs ♪

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:57.734
♪ It's time to take a look
at some Animal Flubs ♪

00:11:57.734 --> 00:12:00.968
♪ From the birds to the bees
to the baby bear cubs ♪

00:12:00.968 --> 00:12:03.300
♪ It's time for Animal Flubs ♪

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:05.701
-F...

00:12:05.701 --> 00:12:07.400
-L...

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:08.667
-U...

00:12:08.667 --> 00:12:11.067
-Aaah!

00:12:11.067 --> 00:12:13.534
-S.

00:12:13.534 --> 00:12:16.968
♪ Are the five letters ♪

00:12:16.968 --> 00:12:20.234
♪ You use ♪

00:12:20.234 --> 00:12:23.334
♪ To spell "flubs" ♪

00:12:23.334 --> 00:12:25.334
♪ Animal Flubs? ♪

00:12:25.334 --> 00:12:26.968
[ Laughter ]

00:12:26.968 --> 00:12:29.033
Cut it down a little, too.
A lot of you said

00:12:29.033 --> 00:12:31.133
you like the long version,
but like two or three people

00:12:31.133 --> 00:12:32.767
said they hated it.
And you know,

00:12:32.767 --> 00:12:36.267
"Corrections" has never been
about making people happy.

00:12:36.267 --> 00:12:41.100
It's always been kowtowing
to the miserable joy-eaters.

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:43.567
[ Laughter ]

00:12:43.567 --> 00:12:46.367
It was time for "Animal Flubs."
You heard 'em.

00:12:46.367 --> 00:12:51.834
Baby koalas are called joeys,
which is dumb.

00:12:51.834 --> 00:12:54.701
I mean, Australia,
why you got to name --

00:12:54.701 --> 00:12:57.200
You got two famous animals,
kangaroos and koalas,

00:12:57.200 --> 00:12:59.968
and both their babies
are called joeys.

00:12:59.968 --> 00:13:02.734
Come on.

00:13:02.734 --> 00:13:04.767
That would be like
someone saying,

00:13:04.767 --> 00:13:06.234
"We should give
Superman a sidekick."

00:13:06.234 --> 00:13:07.667
"Great idea.
What will we call him?"

00:13:07.667 --> 00:13:11.133
"Robin."
[ Laughter ]

00:13:11.133 --> 00:13:14.634
I'm given up on
the pronunciation of "emu."

00:13:14.634 --> 00:13:16.067
Just don't think
it's gonna happen.

00:13:16.067 --> 00:13:18.734
I'm gonna call it
an Australian ostrich.

00:13:18.734 --> 00:13:20.501
A.K.A. "ostrich."

00:13:20.501 --> 00:13:22.434
That doesn't work.

00:13:22.434 --> 00:13:24.634
[ Laughter ]

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:29.467
[ Sighs ]

00:13:29.467 --> 00:13:31.901
Oh, whales aren't --

00:13:31.901 --> 00:13:33.501
Orcas aren't whales.
They're dolphins.

00:13:33.501 --> 00:13:35.033
A lot of people
make that mistake

00:13:35.033 --> 00:13:36.834
'cause they're known
as killer whales.

00:13:36.834 --> 00:13:38.534
They should be called
whale killers.

00:13:38.534 --> 00:13:40.834
Ancient sailors
gave them that name

00:13:40.834 --> 00:13:42.901
because they would see them
sort of hunting

00:13:42.901 --> 00:13:45.234
and preying on larger whales.
And when I was a kid,

00:13:45.234 --> 00:13:47.267
I wished they'd
call them whale killers,

00:13:47.267 --> 00:13:48.667
because at least you know then

00:13:48.667 --> 00:13:49.968
who their victims
are supposed to be.

00:13:49.968 --> 00:13:52.367
And with killer whales,
you don't.

00:13:52.367 --> 00:13:54.400
If they'd been whale killers,
I wouldn't have

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:57.033
[Bleep] my pants
every time I went to SeaWorld.

00:13:57.033 --> 00:14:00.300
[ Laughter ]

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:04.033
This has been "Animal Flubs."

00:14:04.033 --> 00:14:06.601
-♪ Animal Flubs ♪

00:14:06.601 --> 00:14:09.267
-Oh. Oh, God.

00:14:09.267 --> 00:14:11.067
Oh, God, no.

00:14:11.067 --> 00:14:12.968
-What's wrong, Koala?

00:14:12.968 --> 00:14:15.400
-Somebody do something.

00:14:15.400 --> 00:14:18.234
-Aah. Aaah.

00:14:18.234 --> 00:14:20.501
-[ Screams ]
-Seven days.

00:14:20.501 --> 00:14:23.367
[ Laughter ]

00:14:23.367 --> 00:14:25.334
That's the end of it.

00:14:25.334 --> 00:14:26.868
Oh, wait, where'd I go?

00:14:26.868 --> 00:14:28.300
Oh, there I am.

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:31.634
Okay, uh, last but not least,
"Cicada, Cicada" news.

00:14:31.634 --> 00:14:33.834
Good news first.

00:14:33.834 --> 00:14:36.200
We got the prototype
for the cicada's headpiece.

00:14:36.200 --> 00:14:38.367
It looks excellent.

00:14:38.367 --> 00:14:41.367
Bad news.

00:14:41.367 --> 00:14:44.801
It's, like, super [Bleep] hot.

00:14:44.801 --> 00:14:46.467
[ Laughter ]

